WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.031 --> 00:00:07.964
"There is no greatness where simplicity, goodness, and truth are absent," wrote the Russian writer Leo Tolstoy.

2
00:00:07.965 --> 00:00:14.073
With these few words, he expressed a belief that would inspire him for much of his life.

3
00:00:14.074 --> 00:00:31.281
Tolstoy gave the world masterpieces such as War and Peace , an epic novel about Napoleon’s invasion of Russia; Anna Karenina , a tragic tale of forbidden love; and The Death of Ivan Ilyich , a story of spiritual awakening

4
00:00:31.282 --> 00:00:41.394
and physical death. But despite his success, Leo Tolstoy decided in the end to leave his comfortable life behind to live as a

5
00:00:41.395 --> 00:00:54.051
simple peasant. Leo Tolstoy was born on August 28, 1828, at Yasnaya Polyana, his father’s large country estate located 210 kilometers south

6
00:00:54.052 --> 00:01:01.977
of Moscow. Tolstoy’s mother died when he was still an infant, and he became an orphan at age nine when his father died

7
00:01:01.978 --> 00:01:10.252
as well. Tolstoy and his four siblings lived with one relative after another until they settled at the home of a

8
00:01:10.253 --> 00:01:19.515
distant cousin. After several years of studying with private tutors, Tolstoy enrolled at a university in western Russia when he

9
00:01:19.516 --> 00:01:27.500
was 16. But he was more interested in going to parties, and he eventually abandoned his schoolwork in favor of drinking

10
00:01:27.501 --> 00:01:35.945
and gambling. In 1847, nearly penniless, 19-year-old Leo Tolstoy returned home to manage the

11
00:01:35.946 --> 00:01:45.783
family estate. Unfortunately, the family home had been sold to pay his gambling debts, and Tolstoy had to live in a nearby

12
00:01:45.784 --> 00:01:58.590
caretaker’s cottage. On the estate, Tolstoy supervised the serfs—peasants who were forced to work for wealthy families—and decided to educate himself by reading classic works of literature and

13
00:01:58.591 --> 00:02:08.575
philosophy. Despite his newfound desire for wisdom and maturity, his frequent visits to Moscow offered temptations he found hard to

14
00:02:08.576 --> 00:02:13.368
resist. He drank, gambled, and had many brief affairs with

15
00:02:13.369 --> 00:02:26.624
women. "Girls, silly music . . . heat, cigarette smoke, girls, vodka, cheese, screams and shouts, girls, girls, girls!" he noted in the diary he had started to track his

16
00:02:26.625 --> 00:02:36.071
self-improvement. Keeping a diary became a lifelong habit, and Tolstoy regularly recorded both his actions and his judgments about his actions in his

17
00:02:36.072 --> 00:02:44.850
diary. If he had gambled the day before, he would confess it on paper, scold himself, and then make a rule to correct his

18
00:02:44.851 --> 00:02:49.897
behavior. His efforts at self-discipline, however, were not very

19
00:02:49.898 --> 00:02:55.092
successful. "I am not pleased with myself," he wrote in his diary in

20
00:02:55.093 --> 00:03:05.484
1851. Frustrated with his wasteful ways and moral lapses, Tolstoy decided to join an artillery regiment and traveled south to the Caucasus Mountains.

21
00:03:05.485 --> 00:03:12.603
There, he recorded keen observations of military life in his diary and began writing stories.

22
00:03:12.604 --> 00:03:23.701
In 1852, Tolstoy sent a manuscript about his early years, eventually titled Childhood , to the literary journal The Contemporary , which

23
00:03:23.702 --> 00:03:32.241
published it. The Contemporary also published his short stories based on his experiences in the Caucasus, most notably

24
00:03:32.242 --> 00:03:36.184
"The Raid." His work was immediately noticed and

25
00:03:36.185 --> 00:03:43.557
widely praised. "His talent seemed geniuslike, and we were a little timid before it," wrote a reviewer

26
00:03:43.558 --> 00:03:54.718
in 1861. The Crimean War began in 1853, and Tolstoy, stationed in Sevastopol, a port city on the Black Sea, saw firsthand the brutality

27
00:03:54.719 --> 00:04:06.153
of war. He wrote in his book, Sevastopol , in 1856, "In the blooming valley . . . lay disfigured corpses, shoeless, in garments of gray or blue

28
00:04:06.154 --> 00:04:09.655
. . . The odor of the dead bodies

29
00:04:09.656 --> 00:04:18.484
filled the air." Famous now, Tolstoy could have enjoyed a life of celebrity, but he discovered within himself a strict

30
00:04:18.485 --> 00:04:28.923
moral conscience. The seeming indifference of the army officers to the awful experiences of ordinary foot soldiers during the war had given him a dislike

31
00:04:28.924 --> 00:04:41.095
of authority. He could not help but compare the peasants fighting beside him to the ones on his estate, and he came to believe that their simple lives were the only ones with true value

32
00:04:41.096 --> 00:04:47.784
or meaning. Upon returning home, Tolstoy decided to create a school for the serfs working at

33
00:04:47.785 --> 00:04:56.394
Yasnaya Polyana. News about Tolstoy’s school quickly spread. Some people wondered why the great Tolstoy would bother to

34
00:04:56.395 --> 00:05:04.936
educate peasants. He answered his critics by writing an article arguing that even eminent artists had much to learn from peasants

35
00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:12.476
and children. A restless man, Tolstoy traveled frequently, and wherever he went, he was greeted as

36
00:05:12.477 --> 00:05:19.150
a luminary. But he felt uncomfortable with fame and disliked people who considered themselves better than

37
00:05:19.151 --> 00:05:28.874
others. During a trip to Moscow in 1862, Tolstoy met Sofia Behrs, the daughter of a wealthy doctor, and they quickly fell in

38
00:05:28.875 --> 00:05:36.747
love. On the eve of their wedding, Tolstoy gave his diaries to his new wife, saying she must learn everything about

39
00:05:36.748 --> 00:05:44.407
him. The Tolstoys were married for 48 years and had 13 children, 10 of whom survived

40
00:05:44.408 --> 00:05:56.751
infancy. Sofia managed the household, cared for the children, and acted as secretary to Tolstoy, spending long hours copying out his many scrawled manuscripts in her neat

41
00:05:56.752 --> 00:06:07.342
handwriting. Between 1865 and 1869, Tolstoy wrote War and Peace , considered one of the world’s greatest works

42
00:06:07.343 --> 00:06:24.265
of literature. Set in the years 1805 to 1813, the novel tells the stories of five aristocratic Russian families during the Napoleonic Wars, European conflicts that were a result of Napoleon Bonaparte’s desire for a

43
00:06:24.266 --> 00:06:47.704
French empire. In this book, Tolstoy explored two important themes: first, that history is created not just by great events or important people, but rather, by millions of ordinary people making thousands of decisions; and second, that free will, according to Tolstoy, is an illusion since everything is ordained by

44
00:06:47.705 --> 00:06:54.354
God. Anna Karenina , published in 1877, was regarded as

45
00:06:54.355 --> 00:07:03.895
another masterpiece. Tolstoy believed that the novel, about a love affair between a married woman and a dashing military officer, was the best thing he had

46
00:07:03.896 --> 00:07:14.946
ever written. He had come to despise the cruelty of the wealthy and powerful, and he rejected the authority of both the government and society—themes he discussed in

47
00:07:14.947 --> 00:07:24.775
the book. After War and Peace and Anna Karenina were published, the world recognized Tolstoy as a literary giant, and he could have rested on

48
00:07:24.776 --> 00:07:30.535
his fame. But his interest lay in finding spiritual and moral truth in his

49
00:07:30.536 --> 00:07:41.031
own life. Over the years, he had come to believe that the Russian Orthodox Church was corrupt and that all organized Christianity misrepresented Christ’s

50
00:07:41.032 --> 00:07:49.642
message. He wrote numerous essays on his spiritual beliefs, and people came from around the world to visit his estate and discuss his

51
00:07:49.643 --> 00:08:02.251
ideas. His wife expressed unhappiness at Yasnaya Polyana being overrun by strangers, while Tolstoy struggled daily with the desire to give away all his money and live his life in a simple

52
00:08:02.252 --> 00:08:07.804
way. Sofia strongly opposed this idea because she needed money to raise their

53
00:08:07.805 --> 00:08:12.738
children. Tension that would never soften arose between husband and

54
00:08:12.739 --> 00:08:21.510
wife. "We were what stood in the way of his carrying out his dream of a free, new life," Sofia wrote in her

55
00:08:21.511 --> 00:08:34.046
autobiography. In November 1910, the 82-year-old Tolstoy turned his possessions and land over to his family and set out to finally live the life of an ordinary

56
00:08:34.047 --> 00:08:36.591
peasant. But he did not get very

57
00:08:36.592 --> 00:08:45.328
far. Within days, he fell ill, and the world-famous novelist died in a railroad stationmaster’s humble

58
00:08:45.329 --> 00:09:01.645
shack. Because of his relentless search for life’s meaning and his desire to shun his wealth, Tolstoy was misunderstood by many of his colleagues, but they could not deny that the writer had found much wisdom in the daily life he observed around him.

59
00:09:01.646 --> 00:09:12.194
Tolstoy’s novels and short stories are regarded as among the best, most realistic descriptions of the complex lives of human beings to have ever been written.

60
00:09:12.195 --> 00:09:20.571
As the writer Isaac Babel said, "If the world could write by itself, it would write like Tolstoy."

